// Induction over a climbing index (extends ladder rung L7): the counting loop
// walks `index` up to `limit`, adding one per step, so the result covers the
// starting accumulator plus the remaining bounded distance. The induction
// hypothesis enters on the recursive arm because the argumented
// bounded-distance measure `(index, limit) -> Nat::BoundedDistance` is
// discharged at the call site: under the `index < limit` guard the distance
// is strictly smaller at (limit, index + 1) and still non-negative -- the
// same strict-decrease + non-negativity polynomial gate the plain
// descending-naturals measure uses. The instantiated hypothesis for
// (limit, index + 1, acc + 1) is exactly the goal; the base arm closes
// because the negated guard makes the remaining distance non-positive. (The
// inequality form holds without a `requires index <= limit` precondition:
// the arm's own guard supplies that side.)
//
// The `requires` clause supplies the carrier bound, and none of its three
// conjuncts can be dropped. `embed` lifts a runtime carrier's value into
// proof `Int`; it does not re-domain the argument that produced it, so the
// `index + 1` and `acc + 1` stay Exact `u64` operations owing their own
// representability -- "Every Exact operation owes its own representability
// proof in its actual carrier ... A safe final result, mathematical
// substitution, or unknown analysis endpoint cannot excuse an unsafe
// intermediate" (wiki/spec/language/numeric_values.md). `index + 1` narrows
// from the arm's guard; `acc + 1` narrows from `acc <= 1000`, and the
// accumulator invariant `acc <= index` with `limit <= 1000` is what
// re-establishes that bound at the recursive arrival
// (`acc <= index`, `index < limit`, `limit <= 1000`, so `acc + 1 <= 1000`).
// Drop `acc <= 1000` and the transition argument is rejected as unprovable;
// drop `acc <= index` or `limit <= 1000` and the arrival cannot re-establish
// it. The unbounded spelling is the fail canary
// fail/proofs/inductive_climbing_sum_unbounded_accumulator. Neither
// Wrapping nor Saturating substitutes for the bound here: the `ensures` is
// an inequality that a modular reduction or a clamp at `u64::MAX` would
// falsify rather than preserve, which is why the Gauss-sum sibling can be
// modular and this one cannot.

data Main {
}

machine Main::main(&mut self) {
    let total: u64 = self.climb(4, 0, 0);
}

machine Main::climb(&mut self, limit: u64, index: u64, acc: u64) -> u64
requires
    limit <= 1000,
    acc <= index,
    acc <= 1000
terminates by (index, limit) -> Nat::BoundedDistance;
ensures
    embed(result) >= embed(acc) + embed(limit) - embed(index)
{
    transition index < limit {
        true -> climb(limit, index + 1, acc + 1)
        false -> acc
    }
}
